<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Norwegian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norway is a Scandinavian country on the western side of the Scandinavian Peninsula, united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> for much of the early modern period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> won Norwegian provinces including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a> from Denmark-Norway in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> was killed in 1718 while invading Norway, at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norway.jpg" alt="Norway" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Fredrik Meltzer Vector: Gutten på Hemsen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Norway.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Norway's core territory comprises the western and northernmost portion of the Scandinavian Peninsula, together with the island of Jan Mayen and the archipelago of Svalbard. Its total area is 385,207 square kilometres, or 323,808 without Svalbard and Jan Mayen. The rugged coastline is broken by huge fjords and thousands of islands, and the mainland coastline including fjords stretches 28,953 kilometres. Sognefjorden is the world's longest fjord at 204 kilometres. The Scandinavian Mountains form much of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and about 400,000 lakes lie within the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest traces of human occupation lie along the coast, where the ice shelf of the last ice age melted between 11,000 and 8000 BCE. The oldest finds are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> dating from 9500 to 6000 BCE, and between 3000 and 2500 BCE Indo-European farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corded-ware-culture">Corded Ware culture</a> arrived in eastern Norway. Bronze was introduced around 1500 BCE, and the oldest known Norwegian runic inscription dates from the third century. By about the 8th century several small political entities existed, and it has been estimated that there were nine petty realms in Western Norway in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p><p>According to tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> unified them into one kingdom in 872 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Battle of Hafrsfjord</a>, becoming the first king of a united Norway; according to the sagas, many Norwegians left the country to live in Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland. From 1537 to 1814 Norway was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–Norway, and from 1814 to 1905 it was in a personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Its Constitution was adopted on 17 May 1814. Norway was neutral during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and was invaded and occupied by Nazi Germany from April 1940 until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Norway has about 5.6 million inhabitants, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> as its capital and largest city. It is a unitary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>, and its unicameral Parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a>, has 169 representatives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sámi people</a> are indigenous to the Far North, and the Sámi Parliament gives them a certain amount of self-determination. From the 19th century up to the 1970s the government tried to assimilate both the Sámi and the Kven through what is called the Norwegianization process.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1792 Denmark-<b>Norway</b> became the first European state to ban the transatlantic slave trade by law, though the ban only took effect in 1803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A Griffin prince, Eric of Pomerania, became king of Denmark, <b>Norway</b> and Sweden in 1397, and later ended his days as a minor duke in Pomerania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Bremen took part in the Hanseatic League from the thirteenth century, traditionally from 1260, but was an awkward member: it was expelled in 1285 for breaking a blockade of <b>Norway</b> and readmitted only in 1358.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaroslav's children married into the royal houses of France, <b>Norway</b>, Hungary and Byzantium, a sign of the dynasty's standing in Europe; his daughter Anna of Kiev became queen of France as the wife of Henry I. The conversion of Rus' fixed the religious and cultural divide between Orthodox…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Harald Bluetooth (r. c. 958–c. 986) set up the larger Jelling stone, which declares that he “won for himself all Denmark and <b>Norway</b> and made the Danes Christian”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of <b>Norway</b> grew out of the many chiefdoms and petty kingdoms that lined the western Scandinavian coast in the Viking Age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
